1. This License applies to original software, source code, documentation, designs, graphics, and other materials expressly identified as being subject to this License (hereinafter, the "Protected Materials").
2. The Protected Materials are subject to applicable intellectual property laws, including Italian Law No. 633 of April 22, 1941, and relevant European Union legislation.
3. This License applies exclusively to rights owned or lawfully controlled by the Author.
4. Third-party materials, libraries, dependencies, assets, and contributions remain subject to their respective licenses and legal conditions.
5. The inclusion of third-party materials within a project shall not be interpreted as transferring ownership of such materials to the Author.

Article 2 — Ownership and Reservation of Rights

1. All intellectual property rights in the Protected Materials are reserved to their respective lawful rights holders.
2. No ownership, title, or intellectual property interest is transferred to any User by virtue of publication, accessibility, or downloading.
3. No authorization shall be presumed from silence, accessibility, attribution, acknowledgment, or the absence of technical restrictions.
4. Any right not expressly granted under this License remains reserved.
5. Nothing in this Article shall prejudice rights that arise independently under applicable law or the terms of the relevant hosting platform.

Article 3 — Definitions

For the purposes of this License:

a. Author: Ryoeh, acting as the original author or lawful rights holder of the relevant Protected Materials.

b. User: Any individual, organization, company, or other entity accessing or interacting with the Protected Materials.

c. Protected Materials: The original materials expressly identified as being governed by this License.

d. Use: Any exploitation of protected expression beyond the activities expressly permitted under this License or applicable law, including reproduction, adaptation, incorporation, distribution, publication, or commercial exploitation.

e. Authorization: An explicit written permission issued by the Author or an appropriately authorized representative.

f. Derivative Work: Any adaptation, modification, translation, transformation, or other work incorporating copyright-protected expression from the Protected Materials.

g. Commercial Use: Any use intended primarily for commercial advantage, financial gain, or the provision of paid products or services.

h. Third-Party Materials: Any material owned or controlled by a person or entity other than the Author.


TITLE II — PUBLIC ACCESS AND PERMITTED ACTIVITIES

Article 4 — Right of Inspection

1. Any person may access, view, read, and inspect publicly available Protected Materials.
2. Permitted inspection activities include:
   a. Personal inspection;
   b. Educational study;
   c. Technical evaluation;
   d. Understanding the structure and implementation of the software;
   e. Reviewing publicly available documentation.
3. Inspection does not grant permission to independently reuse, reproduce, modify, distribute, or commercially exploit the Protected Materials.
4. Ordinary interaction with publicly accessible websites, demonstrations, and services operated by the Author is permitted through their intended functionality.
5. Access to and interaction with hosting platforms shall remain subject to their respective terms of service and technical permissions.
6. This Article does not prohibit activities that applicable mandatory law expressly permits.

Article 5 — Absence of General Reuse Authorization

1. Public availability shall not be interpreted as a general license to use the Protected Materials.
2. No permission to independently reuse the Protected Materials is granted merely because:
   a. The repository is publicly accessible;
   b. The source code can be viewed;
   c. The User can download, clone, or inspect files;
   d. The User provides attribution;
   e. The intended use is personal, educational, or non-commercial;
   f. The Author does not object to the User's activities.
3. Any reuse beyond the activities permitted under Article 4 requires prior written authorization, except where mandatory law provides otherwise.


TITLE III — RESTRICTED ACTIVITIES

Article 6 — Reproduction

1. Without prior written authorization, no User shall reproduce or copy the Protected Materials, in whole or in substantial part, for independent reuse or exploitation.
2. This restriction is subject to:
   a. Rights expressly granted under the hosting platform's terms of service;
   b. Applicable statutory exceptions;
   c. Other rights that cannot lawfully be excluded.
3. In particular, rights granted by GitHub concerning viewing and native repository-forking functionality remain unaffected.

Article 7 — Modification and Adaptation

Without prior written authorization, no User shall, to the extent such activities are restricted by applicable intellectual property law:

a. Modify or adapt the Protected Materials;

b. Create derivative works based upon copyright-protected expression contained in the Protected Materials;

c. Translate or transform protected source code;

d. Incorporate protected portions into another software project;

e. Remove or alter copyright notices or licensing information;

f. Present modified or derived materials as original works without appropriate authorization.

Nothing in this Article restricts activities that applicable mandatory law expressly permits.

Article 8 — Distribution and Publication

Without prior written authorization, no User shall, to the extent such activities are restricted by applicable intellectual property law:

a. Redistribute the Protected Materials outside the permissions granted by the relevant hosting platform;

b. Publish independent copies of the Protected Materials on third-party repositories, mirrors, or websites;

c. Sublicense or transfer rights in the Protected Materials;

d. Offer the Protected Materials as part of another product or service;

e. Distribute derivative works incorporating protected expression.

Platform-native permissions and mandatory legal exceptions remain unaffected.

Article 9 — Commercial Exploitation

1. Commercial exploitation of the Protected Materials is prohibited unless expressly authorized in writing by the Author, to the extent the relevant activity is controlled by applicable intellectual property rights.
2. Commercial exploitation includes, but is not limited to:
   a. Incorporation into commercial software or products;
   b. Sale or licensing of products incorporating protected expression;
   c. Provision of paid services based upon the Protected Materials;
   d. Distribution for commercial advantage;
   e. Use in commercial applications or services.
3. Authorization for commercial use may be subject to separate conditions, fees, limitations, and written agreements.
4. The absence of financial gain shall not automatically establish that a particular activity is authorized.

Article 10 — Automated Processing, Text and Data Mining, and Machine Learning

1. To the extent permitted by applicable law, the Author expressly reserves the rights necessary to prevent unauthorized text and data mining, automated extraction, and other forms of exploitation of the Protected Materials.
2. This reservation is intended to operate as an express reservation of rights under Article 4 of Directive (EU) 2019/790 and applicable national implementing legislation, where the relevant legal requirements are satisfied.
3. Without prior written authorization, no person or automated agent shall undertake activities involving the Protected Materials for:
   a. Compilation into independently distributed code datasets;
   b. Training, pre-training, validation, or fine-tuning of machine learning models, neural networks, or artificial intelligence systems;
   c. Creation of derivative datasets intended for redistribution or commercial deployment;
   d. Automated extraction or reproduction for independent exploitation.
4. This Article shall not override:
   a. Mandatory legal exceptions;
   b. Rights expressly granted by applicable hosting platform terms;
   c. Activities that applicable law permits notwithstanding a reservation of rights.
5. The Author may implement additional technical measures and machine-readable rights reservations where appropriate.
6. This Article does not represent a guarantee that every automated system will recognize or technically enforce this reservation.


TITLE IV — AUTHORIZATION PROCEDURE

Article 11 — General Requirement

1. Any activity requiring authorization under this License must receive explicit written approval before commencement.
2. Authorization shall not be inferred from:
   a. Submission of a request;
   b. Passage of time;
   c. Absence of an objection;
   d. Informal discussions;
   e. Public availability of the Protected Materials.
3. Each authorization shall be interpreted strictly according to its expressly stated scope.
4. Activities falling outside the scope of an authorization require separate approval.

Article 12 — Official Request Channel

1. Requests for authorization should be submitted through the official GitHub repository or Issues channel designated by the Author:

https://github.com/Ryoeh/ryoeh.github.io/issues

2. Each request should identify:
   a. The Protected Materials concerned;
   b. The intended purpose;
   c. The nature and extent of the proposed use;
   d. Whether the proposed use is commercial;
   e. Whether modifications, incorporation, or redistribution are intended;
   f. The expected duration of the proposed authorization.
3. The Author may request additional information before making a decision.
4. Submission of a request does not constitute authorization.
5. Requests should not contain confidential, sensitive, or personal information that is unnecessary for the evaluation of the proposed use.

Article 13 — Grant of Authorization

1. Authorization shall be valid only when expressly granted in writing by the Author or an appropriately authorized representative.
2. The authorization should specify:
   a. The materials covered;
   b. The permitted activities;
   c. The duration;
   d. The applicable restrictions;
   e. Any financial or non-financial conditions;
   f. Whether redistribution or sublicensing is permitted.
3. Authorization for one purpose shall not imply authorization for another.
4. Authorization shall not be transferable or sublicensable unless expressly stated otherwise.
5. Any material modification to the approved use requires additional written authorization.
6. Where a separate agreement is required by law, the relevant authorization shall be subject to that agreement.

Article 14 — Revocation and Termination

1. Authorizations may be terminated in accordance with their express conditions and applicable law.
2. A material breach of an authorization may constitute grounds for termination, subject to applicable legal requirements.
3. Upon lawful termination, the recipient shall cease the activities covered by the terminated authorization within the applicable period.
4. The recipient shall remove or destroy copies where legally required or reasonably necessary to comply with the termination, subject to mandatory retention obligations and applicable law.
5. Termination shall not automatically invalidate rights or obligations that, by their nature or under applicable law, survive termination.
6. This Article shall not affect rights granted independently by the hosting platform or mandatory statutory rights.


